.PP
The server calls \f[CR]forkpty()\f[R] to create a pseudo\-terminal and
\f[CR]exec()\f[R] the specified shell in the child process.
The parent retains the PTY master file descriptor for I/O relay.
.SH I/O relay loop
After spawning, the server enters a \f[CR]select()\f[R]\-based relay
loop that multiplexes between two sources:
.IP "1." 3
\f[B]Client socket\f[R] \(en Messages from the client (input data,
resize, signal, close)
.IP "2." 3
\f[B]PTY master fd\f[R] \(en Output from the shell process
.PP
Data flow:
.IP
.EX
Client \-\-> CHAN_PTY:input \-\-> write() \-\-> PTY master \-\-> bash
bash \-\-> PTY master \-\-> read() \-\-> CHAN_PTY:output \-\-> Client
.EE
.PP
All data payloads (input and output) are base64\-encoded for binary
safety, since terminal I/O can contain arbitrary bytes including NUL,
control characters, and escape sequences.
.SH Resize
The client sends a \f[CR]resize\f[R] message with new \f[CR]rows\f[R]
and \f[CR]cols\f[R] values.
The server applies the new dimensions using:
.IP "1." 3
\f[CR]TIOCSWINSZ\f[R] ioctl on the PTY master fd to update the terminal
size
.IP "2." 3
\f[CR]SIGWINCH\f[R] sent to the child process group, notifying the shell
and any foreground programs of the size change
.SH Signal injection
The client can inject signals into the PTY child process by sending a
\f[CR]signal\f[R] message with a signal name string:
.IP
.EX
{\(dqtype\(dq: \(dqsignal\(dq, \(dqname\(dq: \(dqSIGINT\(dq}
.EE
.PP
The server parses the signal name via \f[CR]pty_parse_signal()\f[R],
which accepts standard signal names (SIGINT, SIGTERM, SIGKILL, SIGTSTP,
SIGCONT, SIGHUP, SIGQUIT, SIGUSR1, SIGUSR2, etc.)
and sends the signal to the child process.
.SH Session termination
A PTY session ends when:
.IP \(bu 2
The client sends a \f[CR]close\f[R] message
.IP \(bu 2
The child process exits (detected by \f[CR]read()\f[R] returning 0 or
error on the PTY master fd)
.PP
On child exit, the server drains any remaining output from the PTY
master fd, sends it to the client, and then sends an \f[CR]exit\f[R]
message with the child\(cqs exit code:
.IP \(bu 2
Normal exit: \f[CR]WEXITSTATUS(status)\f[R] (the exit code)
.IP \(bu 2
Signal death: \f[CR]128 + signal_number\f[R] (following shell
convention)
.SH Control messages during PTY mode
Even while the PTY relay loop is active, the server continues to handle
control messages on other channels.
Specifically, \f[CR]ping\f[R] and \f[CR]disconnect\f[R] messages on
CHAN_CONTROL are processed during the relay loop, allowing keepalive and
graceful shutdown.
.SH ANSI escape stripping
When \f[CR]strip_ansi\f[R] is set to true in the spawn message, the
server applies a stateful filter to all output read from the PTY master
before forwarding it to the client.
This removes terminal escape sequences, producing clean text suitable
for programmatic consumption.
.PP
The filter is implemented as a state machine
(\f[CR]ansi_strip_state_t\f[R]) with the following states:
.PP
\f[B]NORMAL\f[R]: Default state.
Non\-escape bytes pass through.
Transition to ESC on seeing byte 0x1B, or to CSI on seeing byte 0x9B
(8\-bit CSI).
.PP
\f[B]ESC\f[R]: Entered after 0x1B.
Dispatches based on the next byte: \- \f[CR][\f[R] (0x5B) transitions to
CSI \- \f[CR]]\f[R] (0x5D) transitions to OSC \- \f[CR](\f[R],
\f[CR])\f[R], \f[CR]*\f[R], \f[CR]+\f[R] transitions to CHARSET
(two\-char escape) \- Any other byte completes a two\-character escape
sequence
.PP
\f[B]CSI\f[R] (Control Sequence Introducer): Entered after
\f[CR]ESC [\f[R] or 0x9B.
Consumes parameter bytes (0x30\-0x3F), intermediate bytes (0x20\-0x2F),
and the final byte (0x40\-0x7E).
The entire sequence is discarded.
.PP
\f[B]OSC\f[R] (Operating System Command): Entered after
\f[CR]ESC ]\f[R].
Consumes all bytes until terminated by: \- BEL (0x07) \- ST (String
Terminator): ESC followed by \f[CR]\(rs\f[R] (0x5C)
.PP
OSC sequences typically carry window titles, color definitions, and
other metadata.
.PP
\f[B]OSC_ESC\f[R]: Intermediate state within OSC when 0x1B is seen,
waiting for \f[CR]\(rs\f[R] to complete the ST terminator.
.PP
\f[B]CHARSET\f[R]: Entered after \f[CR]ESC (\f[R], \f[CR]ESC )\f[R],
etc.
Consumes exactly one more byte (the charset designator) and returns to
NORMAL.
.SH ANSI stripping properties
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]In\-place safe\f[R]: Output length is always less than or equal to
input length, so the filter can operate in\-place on a buffer.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Persistent across reads\f[R]: The state machine persists across
\f[CR]read()\f[R] boundaries, correctly handling escape sequences that
are split across multiple reads.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Handles 8\-bit CSI\f[R]: Recognizes the single\-byte 0x9B as
equivalent to the two\-byte ESC [ sequence.
